  "worked_solution": "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x=1\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside $-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<x<-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ To get a value that's inside the required domain, we add $-\\pi $ to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =x+(-\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$",
